{
  "$comment": "How the world is painted. Five styles, and the views they are judged on. Read by `npm run world:paint --prefix frontend`, which writes one image per view per style into world/painterly/, and also writes the runtime copy the app reads at frontend/apps/web/public/world/paint.json. The colours themselves stay in world/render.json: this file says what the paint does to them. Two units appear here and mixing them up is the one way to get this wrong. `mark` is always output pixels, so a mark stays the size of a mark at every zoom, because it belongs to the medium. `wander` and `scale` under boundary, and `reach` under edge, are world-space units, because a coastline belongs to the world: one of those is a pixel of a tile at the zoom the tiles were cut, and it is square, so a wander goes as far north as it does east. What it is worth on the ground is not fixed and shrinks with latitude as the cosine does, from about 1.2 km at the equator to 600 m at sixty degrees. It used to say 1.2 km everywhere and that was wrong in both directions; see decision 0030.",
